 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is fast due to the efficient hash lookup and small MLP. RING-NeRF (using HashNeRF parameters) trains in 25k iterations [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is determined by the hash table size ($2^H$ entries per level) and feature dimension. With $H=19$ and $F=8$, memory is manageable on modern GPUs [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Performance:** Fast preview with `render_factor` 4 or 8 [S1, unofficial]. High fidelity with sufficient iterations and resolution [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast training, low memory footprint, easy to implement.
-*   **Limitations:** Hash collisions can occur, potentially affecting quality. Less robust to out-of-distribution inputs compared to continuous encodings.
-*   **When to use:** When training speed and memory efficiency are priorities, and the scene is bounded.
-*   **When not to use:** For unbounded scenes or when high-frequency details are critical and collisions are a concern.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is significantly faster than standard NeRF due to the efficient hash-based lookup and smaller MLP size [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is determined by the hash grid size and MLP parameters. The hash grid size is $L \times H \times D$, where $L$ is the number of levels, $H$ is the hashmap size, and $D$ is the feature dimension [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Performance:** Trained for 25k iterations with a batch size of $2^{16}$ rays [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast training and rendering, good reconstruction quality for many scenes [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Hash collisions can affect quality; memory usage scales with hashmap size [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When fast training and rendering are prioritized over absolute highest fidelity [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is extremely constrained and hashmap size cannot be reduced, or when highest possible fidelity is required and slower methods are acceptable [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## Background
-HashNeRF was introduced by Müller et al. in "Instant Neural Graphics Primitives with a Multiresolution Hash Encoding" (2022) [S1, unofficial]. It popularized the use of multi-resolution hash grids for neural radiance fields, enabling real-time rendering and fast training. The parameters described here are from the unofficial PyTorch implementation by Yash Bhalgat [S1, unofficial]. RING-NeRF also utilizes similar hash grid configurations [S2].
+HashNeRF parameters are derived from the Instant Neural Graphics Primitives (iNGP) method, which introduced hash-based encoding for neural radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. The specific configuration details are documented in the unofficial HashNeRF-pytorch repository [S1, unofficial] and the RING-NeRF paper, which adapts these parameters for versatile neural fields [S2].
 
